          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong adjoint triangle theorem
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            gives conditions under which, given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            and an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
          %p
            Depending on the specific assumptions, the theorem has several variants. The following gives the most common formulation going back to
            %a{:href => "#dubuc68"} Dubuc (1968)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#huq70"} Huq (1970)
          %h2#statement Statement
          #ATT.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Suppose that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \colon B\to C
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              which has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \,\colon\, C\to B
              with the property that the diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mi U
                    %mi F
                    %mi U
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munderover
                      %mo ⇉
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mi F
                        %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mi U
                        %mi ϵ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi F
                    %mi U
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi ϵ
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F U F U
                    \\;
                    \\underoverset
                    {\epsilon F U}
                    {F U \epsilon}
                    {\rightrightarrows}
                    \\;
                    F U \xrightarrow{\epsilon} 1_B
            %p
              is a pointwise
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
              (i.e.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is of
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent+type"} descent type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              a category with
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+coequalizer"} coequalizers of reflexive pairs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \colon A\to B
              has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              if and only if the composite
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U R
              does.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              The direction “only if” is obvious since adjunctions compose. For “if”, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F'
              be a left adjoint of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U R
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L:B\to A
              to be the pointwise coequalizer of
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                    %mi U
                    %mi F
                    %mi U
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo ′
                        %mi U
                        %mi ϵ
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F' U F U \xrightarrow{F' U \epsilon} F' U
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                    %mi U
                    %mi F
                    %mi U
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo ′
                        %mi U
                        %mi θ
                        %mi U
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                    %mi U
                    %mi R
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                    %mi U
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo ′
                        %mi F
                        %mo ′
                        %mi U
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F' U F U \xrightarrow{F' U \theta U}
                    F' U R F' U \xrightarrow{\epsilon' F' U}
                    F' U
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta:F \to R F'
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mate"} mate
              of the equality
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mi R
                    %mo =
                    %mi U
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U R = U R
              under the adjunctions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\dashv U
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mi U
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F'\dashv U R
          #Monadic.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The hypotheses on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              are satisfied whenever it is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+functor"} monadic
          #RegMono.num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              In fact, it suffices to assume that each counit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mi U
                    %mi b
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon : F U b \to b
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+epimorphism"} regular epimorphism
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#StreetVerity"} (Street-Verity), Lemma 2.1
          %h2#ramifications Ramifications
          %p
            Similarly, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+lifting+theorem"} adjoint lifting theorem
            states conditions on a square of functors in order to ensure the existence of certain adjoints. Since a triangle can be viewed as a square with ‘two sides composed’, it is possible to deduce the adjoint lifting theorem from the adjoint triangle theorem as a corollary.
          %p
            It is also possible to derive the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadicity+theorem"} monadicity theorem
            from the adjoint triangle theorem
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#dubuc68"} Dubuc (1968)
